menu "UI (LVGL)"

config UI_SCREENSHOT_OPEN
    bool "Serve GET /api/screenshot without the web login"
    default n
    help
        Development panels only. The screenshot route normally answers to the same
        Basic-auth login as the rest of /api/*. With this set it answers to anyone on
        the network, so that the person reviewing screens can fetch them by URL
        without holding the panel's password.

        Off by default and deliberately absent from boards/*/sdkconfig.board: set it
        in the local sdkconfig of the bench panel, never in a profile that ships. The
        firmware logs a warning at boot while it is on.

config UI_SCROLL_BENCH
    bool "Serve GET /api/ui/scrollbench: a timed, scripted scroll of a long list"
    default n
    help
        Development panels only. Scrolls 600 synthetic rows of a real list widget at a
        fixed speed for a few seconds and answers with the frame times, split into
        rendering, the flush (PPA rotation), the wait for the DMA2D copy and the list's
        own row recycling. It is how a change to the drawing path is judged: the same
        list, speed and duration on every build (src/ui_scroll_bench.cpp).

        The route answers WITHOUT the web login, like /api/screenshot under
        UI_SCREENSHOT_OPEN, and the firmware says so at WARN on every boot. Set it in the
        local sdkconfig of a bench build, never in a profile that ships.

config UI_DOUBLE_FB
    bool "Compose frames in the DSI panel's second frame buffer, shift scrolled lists"
    default y
    help
        With the rotated panel's direct PPA flush (src/ppa_flush.cpp): LVGL's blocks go
        into the frame buffer that is not on the glass, and the DPI driver switches to it
        when the frame is complete, so nothing tears. A list that scrolls (VList) is then
        moved by DMA2D inside the frame buffer, and LVGL draws only the strip that came
        into view: a scroll frame costs a strip's drawing and ~5 ms of copying instead of
        repainting the whole list (~70 ms here).

        Needs the panel driver to make two DPI frame buffers (num_fbs = 2); with one, or
        with this off, frames are drawn straight into the buffer on the glass, as before.

config UI_SHIFT_PRIORITY
    int "LVGL task priority while a frame shifts a scrolled list (0: unchanged)"
    depends on UI_DOUBLE_FB
    range 0 10
    default 2
    help
        A frame that shifts a list draws only the strip that came into view, a few ms of
        CPU. For those frames the LVGL task is raised to this priority, above the radio's
        decoder and the main loop that share its core at priority 1, so a scroll keeps the
        panel's 60 Hz under load; any other frame goes back to the port's priority. 0 keeps
        LVGL where the port put it.

config UI_M3_TEST_SCREEN
    bool "Put the Material 3 component test page in the menu"
    default n
    help
        Development only. Replaces the service screen's menu slot with a page that
        shows every component of src/ui_m3_widgets.h — buttons, chips, switch,
        slider, list rows, rail items, toast, the wavy line and the type roles — so
        the redesign's building blocks can be judged on the glass and by screenshot
        before a real screen is drawn with them (docs/PLAN_UI_M3_2026-09-24.md, U0).

endmenu
